The module linker invalidates the code cache once per call, not per relocation

The RO service's CRO linker (ldr_ro, cro_helper) invalidates the CPU's code
cache for every 4-byte relocation it writes: System::InvalidateCacheRange,
on every core. FastInterp's InvalidateRange walks its whole block cache (16,384
blocks, 10 MB) each time, because its sets are hashed and an address range
does not map to a set range. Pokemon Sun links 11,065 relocations when it
loads FieldRo (leaving a battle, entering the field) and 10,967 for Battle:
measured on an iPhone 14 Pro in Low Power Mode, 554 and 527 ms inside the
linker, a frozen game and frozen controls. Measured on the host, the walk is
24 us per relocation per core.

An RO call (LoadCRO, UnloadCRO, LinkCRO, UnlinkCRO) now holds its
invalidations back (System::BeginDeferredCacheInvalidation) and applies them
when it returns, sorted and merged, through ARM_Interface::InvalidateCacheRanges:
FastInterp walks its cache once, testing each live block against the ranges by
binary search (0.09 ms for FieldRo's 11,065 on the host); the other backends
keep their per-range invalidation through the default. The call runs to
completion before any guest instruction executes (no async work, no thread
sleep in these handlers), so invalidating at its end is as safe as at each
write. Checked against the per-range walk on 20 random sets of up to 3,000
ranges: the same blocks survive.

